#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406613} :activity/id #uuid "e1be1a0c-7a7b-5288-bec9-333df360e987" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048436 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f6be78-4a73-582f-b9be-ccd54037b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406591} {:answer/id #uuid "4493693b-c02e-5ba8-9c1f-0e602a8b0eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406593} {:answer/id #uuid "9d920b62-e442-5621-8fa9-46a7ba4002db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406596} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a70056-20f6-5d8c-bd45-fe08a274eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406600} {:answer/id #uuid "dac58597-74ad-5c55-82b7-a592a5d0742b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406593} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1825133-7014-5a09-9154-148509062f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79089117-3e5a-51ff-ae41-40a7e2901f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65578" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a06f20a6-d2e4-56ba-a165-46b7fcfbdf95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008d940c-e694-5cf3-8295-360b6cad867c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406590} {:answer/id #uuid "bf267273-4455-5b6e-9339-b99f0be93f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406598} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c281e4-9095-5e8a-ba0f-aabb9383d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406599} {:answer/id #uuid "d474685d-0b20-52ae-90c4-81e995b0ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406601} {:answer/id #uuid "f607a11b-899b-59ab-b189-957fb6465093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406601} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4d9909-2b00-5980-9b7e-95f491417da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "139292f5-27c6-5b2f-8f13-1b15372d0a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65581" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =3 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc02456f-a095-5e41-95ce-6a946f125439"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283fcbb3-8865-526f-9a34-cc2afbcdadd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406592} {:answer/id #uuid "641d46cb-ca02-51d3-a8b9-699328a21c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406594} {:answer/id #uuid "709cd0f7-451f-5ee9-8074-0e603e78ea3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406595} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a219de-60fc-550f-9a26-7578926916c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406597} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0bccfb-38e4-51f1-8863-481369f3db5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406592} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d8f167-e779-56fd-836e-ab6aac4f2070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e58d0138-9eca-5984-8b18-90ecf02f0d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11294" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe07e852-ca7d-55ad-9fb8-dab24d09b6c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406611}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406589}]}
